            <div xml:id="d334">

                <pb facs="0497" n="183" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg183"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1023</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American
                    Legation</hi>,<placeName>Peking</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-10-02T01:00:00+08:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">October 2, 1914, 1 a.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received October 1, 6.30 p m</hi>.] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>As the result of a long Cabinet meeting to-day Liang Tun Yen informs me that the
                    Chinese Government, while accepting in good, faith the assurances of Japan and
                    not intending to offer any opposition to her operations, is deeply concerned
                    over the proposed military occupation of the Shantung Railway west of Weihsien
                    fearing that such penetration into the interior away from the seat of war will
                    almost inevitably lead to incidents beyond its control threatening to force
                    China into hostilities. President Yuan will, therefore, through Minister Shah
                    ask President Wilson to confer with the British Government as to the possibility
                    of prevailing upon. Japan to restrict her action to the necessary field of
                    belligerent operations. The seriousness of the situation is generally felt
                    here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Reinsch</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
